# TRIP_ACCESS_CODE and TRIP_GUEST_CODE are gone. They were shared codes that
# reached every trip in the database, which no per-trip membership can
# constrain — a code is a right, not a person. Everyone signs in with an
# account now (below), and friends are invited to a trip as viewers, with
# per-member control over whether they see the stays, the flight and the
# documents. Delete both from any deployment that still sets them.

# Data backend: "memory" (placeholder data, no infra needed) | "supabase" (Phase 8)
DATA_BACKEND=memory

# --- Supabase backend (set DATA_BACKEND=supabase) ---
# SUPABASE_URL=https://YOUR-PROJECT-REF.supabase.co
# SUPABASE_SECRET_KEY=sb_secret_...   # the "secret" API key (Settings → API); server-only, bypasses RLS, never shipped to the browser
# (legacy projects: SUPABASE_SERVICE_KEY with the service_role JWT also works)

# --- Account sign-in via Supabase Auth (Google OAuth, email + password, magic-link) ---
# Required: with these unset there is no way into the app at all.
# Registration is open: anyone can create an account, and sees nothing until
# they create a trip or are invited to one. There is no allow-list — the old
# TRIP_OWNER_EMAILS var was replaced by the trip_members table and is ignored.
#
# Google sign-in needs the provider enabled in the Supabase dashboard
# (Authentication → Providers → Google) with the OAuth client's redirect set to
# https://YOUR-PROJECT-REF.supabase.co/auth/v1/callback. Nothing is needed here:
# the browser talks to Supabase directly and the API only ever verifies the
# resulting JWT. With the provider off, the button is simply disabled.
#
# Needed regardless of DATA_BACKEND — Supabase Auth is a property of the
# Supabase project, not of which datastore is selected. SUPABASE_URL (above)
# is reused; the publishable key is safe to also expose to the frontend as
# VITE_SUPABASE_PUBLISHABLE_KEY (see src/lib/supabaseClient.ts).
# SUPABASE_PUBLISHABLE_KEY=sb_publishable_...
# VITE_SUPABASE_URL=https://YOUR-PROJECT-REF.supabase.co
# VITE_SUPABASE_PUBLISHABLE_KEY=sb_publishable_...

# --- PostHog product analytics (browser) ---
# Entirely optional: with these unset nothing is sent, no network call is made
# and the app behaves as it did before analytics existed. Set them to turn it
# on (locally in .env.local, in production as Vercel env vars).
#
# The key is the *public* project token (phc_...), safe to ship to the browser
# — not a personal API key (phx_...), which must never leave a server.
# Either name works: VITE_POSTHOG_PROJECT_TOKEN (PostHog's docs) is read first,
# VITE_POSTHOG_KEY (what its wizard writes) second.
# VITE_POSTHOG_PROJECT_TOKEN=phc_...
# Region host: https://us.i.posthog.com or https://eu.i.posthog.com.
# Defaults to the US host when unset.
# VITE_POSTHOG_HOST=https://us.i.posthog.com

# --- Chat (feature 005) ---
# Optional at runtime, and the switch that matters. With ANTHROPIC_API_KEY
# unset every chat endpoint answers 404: the feature is *absent*, not broken —
# exactly as push is with no VAPID keys above. The `chat-bot` PostHog flag
# hides the button and the route, but a client flag controls no spend, so the
# key is the real control.
#
# Chat is limited to owners and partners. A viewer gets 403 and is offered no
# way in; a non-member gets 404, which does not confirm the trip exists.
# ANTHROPIC_API_KEY=sk-ant-...

# --- PostHog on the server (LLM observability + backend feature flags) ---
# Optional, like everything else here. With no key nothing is reported and every
# flag reads as unanswered, which lands on the values below.
#
# Two things the browser's PostHog cannot do:
#   * report what a model call cost ($ai_generation)
#   * answer a flag the *server* has to act on — a posthog-js flag is evaluated
#     in the browser and can only hide a button
#
# Same project token as VITE_POSTHOG_PROJECT_TOKEN; that one is read as a
# fallback, so setting it twice is optional.
# POSTHOG_PROJECT_API_KEY=phc_...
# POSTHOG_HOST=https://us.i.posthog.com
#
# Flags it reads, both falling back to the values below when PostHog has no
# answer:
#   ai-chat-model         variant or string payload, e.g. anthropic/claude-sonnet-5
#                         A name that is not in the catalogue is ignored.
#   ai-monthly-cap-cents  number payload, the per-account monthly cap
#
# What a flag CANNOT do is unlock something the code does not already support:
# an unknown model falls back rather than being passed through.
#
# NOT reported, deliberately: the prompt and the completion. The prompt is the
# whole trip, booking reference and shopping list included (FR-029).

# Spend caps, in cents, counted from real token usage and summed per calendar
# month. The monthly cap is also a feature flag (above); this is its fallback.
#
# AI_MONTHLY_CAP_CENTS is per account — a person with three trips has one
# budget. At 80% the app says so; at 100% the composer disables with a resume
# date and the transcript stays readable.
#
# AI_GLOBAL_CAP_CENTS is the kill switch across every account, so one runaway
# cannot become the whole bill.
#
# Note the one thing neither can do: usage is only known *after* a turn while
# the check runs *before* it, so a single turn can cross a cap. That is what
# AI_MAX_OUTPUT_TOKENS bounds.
# AI_MONTHLY_CAP_CENTS=1000
# AI_GLOBAL_CAP_CENTS=5000

# Abuse bounds rather than budgeting. AI_MAX_ITERATIONS caps the model loop —
# each web-search pause spends one, and a turn that stops at the bound says the
# answer is incomplete rather than presenting a truncated one as finished.
# AI_MAX_ITERATIONS=5
# AI_MAX_OUTPUT_TOKENS=2048
# AI_DAILY_TURN_LIMIT=100
